      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y+z &=3 \\\\ z &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that $z=6.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(6)=3\\,⟹\\,y=3-(6)=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $y=-3$ and $z=6$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(-3)+(6)=\\frac{3}{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}-(-3)-(6)=- \\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=- \\frac{3}{2}$, $y=-3$, and $z=6.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨- \\frac{3}{2},-3,6⟩.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
